# walls-probe — the outer wall ring on its own, for clipcheck. Not part of the building.
#
#   bash tools/clipcheck.sh clips/facility/requests/walls-probe.clip --metre 32 --gap z@0,3.0
#   bash tools/clipcheck.sh clips/facility/requests/walls-probe.clip --metre 32 --slice y@3.0
#
# It exists so the wall can be MEASURED without the other twenty-odd fragments' paint rules being
# asked about every voxel of it: `--part part_walls` on the manifest samples the same stone and
# then evaluates every other fragment's coats over it, which is a three-and-a-half-minute run at
# metre 8 where this is seconds. It also makes `never fired` mean something, which it cannot when
# nineteen other files' rules are in the list.
#
# THE BOX IS PHASE-ALIGNED TO THE CONTRACT'S, which is the whole reason it is these numbers and not
# round ones. The contract's box starts at (-17, -0.9, -16.5); this one starts at (-17, 1.6, -8.5),
# which is 0.00, 2.50 and 8.00 m away — a whole number of voxels at metre 8, 16 and 32 alike. So
# every voxel centre falls exactly where it falls in the building, and a 0.03 m feature that
# survives here survives there. A box chosen for tidiness would shift the grid by a fraction of a
# voxel and quietly change which one-voxel members exist.
#
# It holds the whole part with margin: the wall is x -16.135..16.135 (quoins), y 1.75..11.90,
# z -7.68..7.68 (the string course mitre), so anything that had drifted would fall OUT of this box
# and be seen rather than hidden.
include "../_contract.clip"
include "../_order.clip"

paint limestone

include "../walls.clip"

metre 32
bounds -17 1.6 -8.5   17 12.5 8.5

variation colour=0.030 rough=0.070 seed=17

solid part_walls
